{"title":"MHT CET","category":"Engineering","bank":"mht_cet","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Equilibrium","count":21,"questions":[{"id":55948,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"Calculate $\\triangle G^\\circ$ for reaction,<br>$\\text{CH}_{4(g)} + \\text{H}_{2(g)} \\rightarrow \\text{C}_2\\text{H}_{6(g)}$ ($K_p = 2 \\times 10^{17}$, $R = 8.314\\ \\text{JK}^{-1}\\text{mol}^{-1}$)","options":["$-64.695$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-98.716$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-44.08$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-58.78$ kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"The standard Gibbs free energy change is related to the equilibrium constant by the equation:<br><br>$\\Delta G^\\circ = -RT \\ln K_p = -2.303 RT \\log K_p$<br><br>Assuming standard temperature $T = 298 \\text{ K}$ and substituting the given values:<br><br>$\\Delta G^\\circ = -2.303 \\times 8.314 \\times 298 \\times \\log(2 \\times 10^{17})$<br><br>$\\Delta G^\\circ = -5705.84 \\times (\\log 2 + 17 \\log 10)$<br><br>$\\Delta G^\\circ = -5705.84 \\times (0.3010 + 17)$<br><br>$\\Delta G^\\circ = -5705.84 \\times 17.3010$<br><br>$\\Delta G^\\circ = -98716 \\text{ J mol}^{-1}$<br><br>$\\Delta G^\\circ = -98.716 \\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$<br><br>Answer: $-98.716$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"MHT CET 2026 (20 April Shift 1)","_marksId":"6a44d3518c30a4553e1be276"},{"id":55949,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"Find $\\Delta n$ when 1 mol of each $\\text{NH}_{3(g)}$ and $\\text{HCl}_{(g)}$, reacts to form solid $\\text{NH}_4\\text{Cl}$.","options":["$1$","$-2$","$-1$","$-3$"],"answer":1,"solution":"The balanced chemical equation for the reaction is:<br><br>$\\text{NH}_{3(g)} + \\text{HCl}_{(g)} \\rightarrow \\text{NH}_4\\text{Cl}_{(s)}$<br><br>The change in the number of moles of gaseous species, $\\Delta n$, is given by the difference between the number of moles of gaseous products and gaseous reactants.<br><br>Number of moles of gaseous products, $n_P = 0$<br><br>Number of moles of gaseous reactants, $n_R = 1 + 1 = 2$<br><br>$\\Delta n = n_P - n_R = 0 - 2 = -2$<br><br>Answer: $-2$","difficulty":"Easy","source":"MHT CET 2026 (15 April Shift 2)","_marksId":"6a44d2e28c30a4553e1bddd2"},{"id":55950,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"Identify from following salts so that the solubility of salt in water decreases with increase in temperature.","options":["NaBr","NaCl","$\\mathrm{NaNO}_3$","$\\mathrm{Na}_2 \\mathrm{SO}_4$"],"answer":3,"solution":"<p>Most ionic compounds exhibit increased solubility in water with rising temperature due to the endothermic nature of their dissolution process. Applying Le Chatelier's principle, increasing temperature shifts the equilibrium toward the dissolved ions, enhancing solubility.</p><p>Exceptions occur when dissolution is exothermic. For these salts, elevated temperature shifts equilibrium toward the solid form, reducing solubility.</p><p>Sodium bromide and sodium chloride solubility increases with temperature, while sodium nitrate shows significant temperature-dependent solubility enhancement.</p><p>Sodium sulfate displays unique behavior: below 32.38 °C, its decahydrate form solubility increases with temperature, but above this transition point, the dissolution of anhydrous $\\mathrm{Na}_2\\mathrm{SO}_4$ becomes exothermic, causing solubility to decrease with increasing temperature.</p><p><strong>Final Answer:</strong> $\\boxed{\\text{D}}$</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"MHT CET 2025 (19 Apr Shift 2)","_marksId":"68871b12bf9280d7f9932609"},{"id":55951,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"A weak base is $1.42 \\%$ dissociated in its $0.05 \\mathrm{M}$ solution. Calculate its dissociation constant.","options":["$5.5 \\times 10^{-5}$","$4.0 \\times 10^{-5}$","$1.8 \\times 10^{-5}$","$1.0 \\times 10^{-5}$"],"answer":3,"solution":"Percent dissociation $=1.42 \\%\\therefore \\quad \\alpha=0.0142$\nFor a weak monoacidic base,\n$\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{b}} & =\\alpha^2 \\mathrm{C} \\\\\n& =(0.0142)^2 \\times(0.05) \\\\\n& =1.0082 \\times 10^{-5}\n\\end{aligned}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"MHT CET 2023 (11 May Shift 2)","_marksId":"65a2620e0279619d9d8a3174"},{"id":55952,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"Calculate the degree of dissociation of $0.01 \\mathrm{M}$ acetic acid at $25^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}\\left[\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{c}}=15.0 \\Omega^{-1} \\mathrm{~cm}^2 \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}\\right.$ and $\\left.\\Lambda_0=300 \\Omega^{-1} \\mathrm{~cm}^2 \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}\\right]$","options":["$0.042$","$0.035$","$0.025$","$0.05$"],"answer":3,"solution":"Degree of dissociation $(\\alpha)=\\frac{\\Lambda_c}{\\Lambda_0}=\\frac{15.0}{300}=0.05$","difficulty":"Easy","source":"MHT CET 2023 (11 May Shift 2)","_marksId":"65a2620f0279619d9d8a317c"},{"id":55953,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"A weak monobasic acid dissociates to $0.001 \\%$ in its $0.01 \\mathrm{M}$ solution. What is its dissociation constant?","options":["$1 \\times 10^{-6}$","$1 \\times 10^{-8}$","$1 \\times 10^{-3}$","$1 \\times 10^{-12}$"],"answer":3,"solution":"$\\begin{aligned} & \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}=\\propto^2 \\mathrm{C} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{0.001}{100}\\right)^2 \\times 0.01 \\\\ & =1 \\times 10^{-12}\\end{aligned}$","difficulty":"Easy","source":"MHT CET 2022 (08 Aug Shift 2)","_marksId":"65a261650279619d9d8a2c36"},{"id":55954,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"The percent dissociation of weak monobasic acid is $3 \\%$ in its $0.02 \\mathrm{M}$ solution. What is the dissociation constant of acid?","options":["$9 \\times 10^{-4}$","$3 \\times 10^{-2}$","$2 \\times 10^{-2}$","$1.8 \\times 10^{-5}$"],"answer":3,"solution":"$\\begin{aligned} & K_a=\\propto^2 C \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{3}{100}\\right)^2 \\times 0.02 \\\\ & =1.8 \\times 10^{-5}\\end{aligned}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"MHT CET 2022 (08 Aug Shift 1)","_marksId":"65a261500279619d9d8a2b90"},{"id":55955,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"A weak monoacidic base is $1.2 \\%$ dissociated in its $0.2 \\mathrm{M}$ solution. What is the value of dissociation constant?","options":["$1.21 \\times 10^{-5}$","$1.44 \\times 10^{-5}$","$2.54 \\times 10^{-5}$","$2.88 \\times 10^{-5}$"],"answer":3,"solution":"$\\begin{aligned} & \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{b}}=\\propto^2 \\mathrm{C} \\\\ & =(0.012)^2 \\times 0.2 \\\\ & =2.88 \\times 10^{-5}\\end{aligned}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"MHT CET 2022 (07 Aug Shift 2)","_marksId":"65a2613f0279619d9d8a2b00"},{"id":55956,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"A weak monobasic acid is $2 \\%$ dissociated in its $0.1 \\mathrm{M}$ solution. What is its dissociation constant?","options":["$4.0 \\times 10^{-5}$","$1.0 \\times 10^{-5}$","$2.0 \\times 10^{-5}$","$2.5 \\times 10^{-5}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"$\\begin{aligned} & \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}=\\propto^2 \\mathrm{C} \\\\ & =(0.02)^2 \\times 0.1 \\\\ & =4 \\times 10^{-5}\\end{aligned}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"MHT CET 2022 (07 Aug Shift 1)","_marksId":"65a2612c0279619d9d8a2a67"},{"id":55957,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"An acid dissociated to $1.5 \\%$ in its $0.1 \\mathrm{M}$ solution. Calculate its dissociation constant.","options":["$1.2 \\times 10^{-5}$","$2.25 \\times 10^{-5}$","$1.1 \\times 10^{-5}$","$1.5 \\times 10^{-5}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\begin{aligned} & K_a=\\alpha^2 C \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1.5}{100}\\right)^2 \\times 0.1 \\\\ & =2.25 \\times 10^{-5}\\end{aligned}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"MHT CET 2022 (06 Aug Shift 2)","_marksId":"65a2611b0279619d9d8a29de"},{"id":55958,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"An organic weak monobasic acid is 0.001 percent dissociated in its $0.05 \\mathrm{M}$ solution. What is its dissociation constant?","options":["$6 \\times 10^{-12}$","$5 \\times 10^{-12}$","$4 \\times 10^{-12}$","$1 \\times 10^{-12}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\begin{aligned} & \\mathrm{Ka}=\\alpha^2 \\mathrm{C} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{0.001}{100}\\right)^2 \\times 0.05 \\\\ & =5 \\times 10^{-12}\\end{aligned}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"MHT CET 2022 (06 Aug Shift 1)","_marksId":"65a2610a0279619d9d8a2953"},{"id":55959,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"At $298 \\mathrm{~K}, 0.1 \\mathrm{M}$ solution of acetic acid is $1.34 \\%$ ionized. What is the dissociation constant of acetic acid?","options":["$1.4 \\times 10^{-3}$","$1.8 \\times 10^{-5}$","$1.6 \\times 10^{-3}$","$1.34 \\times 10^{-5}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fmht_cet%252FgGYM_oXlcH8vwKSfz72k2vEVvNHSLBApn-ihgSeS4dQ.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fmht_cet%2FgGYM_oXlcH8vwKSfz72k2vEVvNHSLBApn-ihgSeS4dQ.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fmht_cet%2FgGYM_oXlcH8vwKSfz72k2vEVvNHSLBApn-ihgSeS4dQ.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"MHT CET 2021 (24 Sep Shift 1)","_marksId":"65a260bc0279619d9d8a26e6"},{"id":55960,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"A weak monobasic acid is $3.0 \\%$ dissociated in it's $0.04 \\mathrm{M}$ solution. What is the dissociation constant of acid?","options":["$9 \\times 10^{-4}$","$3.6 \\times 10^{-5}$","$3 \\times 10^{-2}$","$4 \\times 10^{-2}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fmht_cet%252FDhcH9U2X6w_eNt--GoZYCVZrEV2VYD58p1BcdG3lzIc.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fmht_cet%2FDhcH9U2X6w_eNt--GoZYCVZrEV2VYD58p1BcdG3lzIc.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fmht_cet%2FDhcH9U2X6w_eNt--GoZYCVZrEV2VYD58p1BcdG3lzIc.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"MHT CET 2021 (23 Sep Shift 2)","_marksId":"65a260a70279619d9d8a2640"},{"id":55961,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"What is percent dissociation of acetic acid in it's $0.01 \\mathrm{M}$ solution if dissociation constant of acid is $1.34 \\times 10^{-6}$ ?","options":["$13.4 \\%$","$4.02 \\%$","$2.68 \\%$","$1.15 \\%$"],"answer":3,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fmht_cet%252FZ7B4kJe8D5753J7n91ev93l9onKLLz9MS-VjDQ5DLH0.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fmht_cet%2FZ7B4kJe8D5753J7n91ev93l9onKLLz9MS-VjDQ5DLH0.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fmht_cet%2FZ7B4kJe8D5753J7n91ev93l9onKLLz9MS-VjDQ5DLH0.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\n$\\begin{aligned} & \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{C} \\alpha^2}{1-\\alpha} \\quad 1-\\alpha \\simeq 1 \\\\ & \\mathrm{~K}_{\\mathrm{a}}=\\mathrm{Co}^2 \\\\ & \\alpha=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}}{\\mathrm{C}}} \\\\ & \\% \\alpha=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}}{\\mathrm{C}}} \\times 100=\\sqrt{\\frac{1.34 \\times 00^{-6}}{0.01}} \\times 100 \\\\ & =1.15 \\% \\\\ & \\end{aligned}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"MHT CET 2021 (23 Sep Shift 1)","_marksId":"65a260970279619d9d8a25bf"},{"id":55962,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"The $\\mathrm{pH}$ of monoacidic weak base is 10.9. Calculate the percent dissociation in $0.02 \\mathrm{M}$ solution.","options":["$7.92 \\%$","$3.95 \\%$","$6.25 \\%$","$2.51 \\%$"],"answer":1,"solution":"Monoacidic weak base $\\mathrm{BOH}$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\mathrm{BOH}_{(\\mathrm{aq})} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{B}_{(\\mathrm{aq})}^{+}+\\mathrm{OH}_{(\\mathrm{aq})}^{-} \\\\\n& \\mathrm{C} \\quad \\mathrm{C} \\alpha \\quad \\mathrm{C} \\alpha \\\\\n& \\mathrm{C} \\alpha \\quad- \\\\\n& \\mathrm{pH}=10.9 \\\\\n& \\mathrm{pH}+\\mathrm{pOH}=14 \\\\\n& \\mathrm{pOH}=14-10.9=3.1 \\\\\n& \\mathrm{pOH}=-\\log _{10}\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right] \\\\\n& {\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]=10^{-\\mathrm{pOH}}} \\\\\n& =10^{-3.1}=10^{-4}+0.9 \\\\\n& =10^{-4} \\times 10^{0.9} \\\\\n& {\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]=7.9 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{M}} \\\\\n& {\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]=\\mathrm{C}_\\alpha} \\\\\n& \\alpha=\\frac{7.9 \\times 10^{-4}}{0.02} \\\\\n& \\alpha=3.95 \\times 10^{-2} \\\\\n& \\% \\alpha=3.95 \\times 10^{-2} \\times 100 \\\\\n& =3.95 \\%\n\\end{aligned}\n","difficulty":"Medium","source":"MHT CET 2021 (23 Sep Shift 1)","_marksId":"65a260990279619d9d8a25cf"},{"id":55963,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"A weak monobasic acid is $10 \\%$ dissociated in $0.05 \\mathrm{M}$ solution. What is its percentage dissociation in $0.10 \\mathrm{M}$ solution?","options":["$5.27 \\%$","$7.17 \\%$","$10.3 \\%$","$4.5 \\%$"],"answer":1,"solution":"Weak monobasic acid (HA)\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\underset{\\mathrm{C}}{\\mathrm{HA}_{(\\mathrm{aq})}} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{H}_{\\mathrm{aq}}^{+}+\\mathrm{A}_{\\mathrm{aq}}^{-} \\\\\n& \\text {C-C } \\quad \\mathrm{C} \\propto \\quad \\mathrm{C} \\propto \\\\\n& \\mathrm{Ka}=\\frac{\\mathrm{C} \\propto^2}{1-\\propto} \\\\\n& 1-\\propto \\simeq 1 \\\\\n& \\left(\\begin{array}{l}\n\\mathrm{C}_1=0.05, \\propto=10 \\%=0.1 \\\\\n\\mathrm{C}_2=0.1, \\propto_2=?\n\\end{array}\\right. \\\\\n& \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}=\\mathrm{C}^2 \\\\\n& \\mathrm{C}_1 \\propto_1^2=\\mathrm{C}_2 \\propto \\boldsymbol{c}_2^2 \\\\\n& 0.05 \\times 0.1 \\times 0.1=0.1 \\times \\propto_2^2 \\\\\n& \\alpha_2^2=5 \\times 10^{-3} \\\\\n& =\\frac{5}{1000}=\\frac{1}{200} \\\\\n& \\propto_2=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{200}}=0.717 \\\\\n& \\propto_2=7.17 \\% \\\\\n&\n\\end{aligned}\n","difficulty":"Medium","source":"MHT CET 2021 (22 Sep Shift 2)","_marksId":"65a260860279619d9d8a2533"},{"id":55964,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"The dissociation constant of weak monobasic acid is $2.7 \\times 10^{-5}$. If degree of dissociation of acid is $3 \\times 10^{-2}$, what is the concentration of acid?","options":["0.24 M","0.03 M","0.3 M","0.11 M"],"answer":1,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fmht_cet%252FdQtsdP-QtS0iSmXWSguZ7947-BpxXqV0Hz0swgrkyeY.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fmht_cet%2FdQtsdP-QtS0iSmXWSguZ7947-BpxXqV0Hz0swgrkyeY.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fmht_cet%2FdQtsdP-QtS0iSmXWSguZ7947-BpxXqV0Hz0swgrkyeY.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>","difficulty":"Easy","source":"MHT CET 2021 (22 Sep Shift 1)","_marksId":"65a260740279619d9d8a24a3"},{"id":55965,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"Dissociation constant of propionic acid is $1.32 \\times 10^{-5}$. Calculate the degree of dissociation of acid in $0.05 \\mathrm{M}$ solution.","options":["$2.6 \\times 10^{-4}$","$1.61 \\times 10^{-2}$","$1.90 \\times 10^{-2}$","$3.5 \\times 10^{-5}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\mathrm{CH}_3 \\mathrm{CH}_2 \\mathrm{COOH}$ (Propionic Acid)\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\mathrm{CH}_3 \\mathrm{CH}_2 \\mathrm{COOH}_{(\\mathrm{aq})} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{CH}_3 \\mathrm{CH}_2 \\mathrm{COO}_{(\\mathrm{aq})}^{-}+\\mathrm{H}_{(\\mathrm{aq})}^{+} \\\\\n& \\mathrm{t}=0_1 \\quad \\mathrm{C} \\\\\n& \\mathrm{t}=\\mathrm{t}_{\\mathrm{aq}}, \\quad \\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{C} \\propto \\quad \\mathrm{C} \\propto \\quad \\mathrm{C} \\propto \\\\\n& \\mathrm{k}_{\\mathrm{a}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{c} \\propto^2}{1-\\propto} \\quad(1-\\propto \\simeq 1) \\\\\n&\n\\end{aligned}\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\propto=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mathrm{k}_{\\mathrm{a}}}{\\mathrm{C}}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1.32 \\times 10^{-5}}{0.05}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{132 \\times 10^{-1}}{5}} \\times 10^{-2} \\quad \\mathrm{k}_{\\mathrm{a}}=\\mathrm{c}^2 \\\\\n& =\\sqrt{2.64} \\times 10^{-2}=1.61 \\times 10^{-2}\n\\end{aligned}\n","difficulty":"Hard","source":"MHT CET 2021 (21 Sep Shift 1)","_marksId":"65a260510279619d9d8a2384"},{"id":55966,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"The $\\mathrm{pH}$ of $0.1 \\mathrm{M}$ solution of monobasic acid is 2.34 . Calculate the degree of dissociation of the acid.","options":["$3.1 \\times 10^{-2}$","$4.5 \\times 10^{-2}$","$2.18 \\times 10^{-2}$","$2.5 \\times 10^{-3}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"\n\\mathrm{pH}=2.34, \\mathrm{c}=0.1 \\mathrm{M}\n\n(i) $\\mathrm{pH}=-\\log _{10}\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]$\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\log _{10}\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]=-\\mathrm{pH}=-2.34 \\\\\n=-2-0.34-1+1 \\\\\n=-3+0.66=\\overline{3} .66\n\\end{gathered}\n\n\\therefore\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]=\\text {antilog } \\overline{3} .66\n\n=4.571 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{dm}^{-3}\n\n(ii) $\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]=\\mathrm{c} \\alpha$\n\\therefore \\alpha=\\frac{\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]}{\\mathrm{c}}=\\frac{4.571 \\times 10^{-3}}{0.1}=4.571 \\times 10^{-2}\n","difficulty":"Easy","source":"MHT CET 2021 (20 Sep Shift 2)","_marksId":"65a2603c0279619d9d8a22d8"},{"id":55967,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"What is molar concentration of weak monobasic acid if dissociation constant is $5 \\times 10^{-8}$ and undergoes $0.5 \\%$ dissociation?","options":["$0.03 \\mathrm{M}$","$0.002 \\mathrm{M}$","$0.001 \\mathrm{M}$","0.005"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\begin{aligned} & \\text { Weak monobasic acid } \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{HA} \\\\ & \\mathrm{t}=0, \\mathrm{CA} \\\\ & \\mathrm{t}, \\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{cx} \\\\ & \\text { Dissociation Constant }(\\mathrm{K})=\\frac{\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{A}^{-}\\right]}{[\\mathrm{HA}]}+\\mathrm{A}_{\\mathrm{aq}}^{-} \\\\ & =\\frac{\\mathrm{cx} \\cdot \\mathrm{cx}}{\\mathrm{c}-\\mathrm{cx}} \\\\ & \\mathrm{K}=\\frac{\\mathrm{cx}}{1-\\mathrm{x}} \\\\ & \\mathrm{x}=0.5 \\% \\text { or } 0.005=5 \\times 10^{-3} \\\\ & \\text { So } 1-\\mathrm{x} \\simeq 1 \\\\ & \\mathrm{~K}=\\mathrm{cx} \\\\ & \\mathrm{C}=\\frac{\\mathrm{k}}{\\mathrm{x}^2}=\\frac{5 \\times 10^{-8}}{\\left(5 \\times 10^{-3}\\right)^2}=\\frac{10^{-2}}{5} \\\\ & =0.002 \\mathrm{M}\\end{aligned}$","difficulty":"Easy","source":"MHT CET 2021 (20 Sep Shift 1)","_marksId":"65a2602c0279619d9d8a2258"},{"id":55968,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"A chemical reaction takes places in two steps as follows:<br><br>(i) <math><mi>N</mi><msub><mrow><mi>O</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mn> </mn></mrow><mrow><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mover accent=\"false\"><mo>→</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mover><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mn> </mn><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mn> </mn></mrow><mrow><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow></math><br><br>(ii) <math> <mrow> <mi>N</mi><msub> <mi>O</mi> <mn>2</mn> </msub> <mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo> <mi>g</mi> <mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo> <mi>g</mi> <mo>)</mo></mrow><mover> <mo>→</mo> <mrow> <msub> <mi>K</mi> <mn>2</mn> </msub> </mrow> </mover> <mi>N</mi><msub> <mi>O</mi> <mn>2</mn> </msub> <mrow><mo>(</mo> <mi>g</mi> <mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi><msub> <mi>l</mi> <mn>2</mn> </msub> <mrow><mo>(</mo> <mi>g</mi> <mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow> </math><br><br>Identify the reaction intermediate.","options":["<math><mi>N</mi><msub><mrow><mi>O</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo> </mo></mrow><mrow><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow></msub></math>","<math><mi>N</mi><msub><mrow><mi>O</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo> </mo><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow></math>","<math><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow><mi>l</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo> </mo><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow></msub></math>"